rname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139534637 true] [17592186300206 1113 #uuid "379e9498-17d7-5258-a37f-d94516f74aac" 13194139534637 true] [17592186300206 1145 "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300208 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300209 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300210 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300211 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300212 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1041 "selection" 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1104 #edb/ref 17592186300210 13194139534637 true] [17592186300207 1110 #uuid "2b4a1100-5b8a-5db0-9078-e78b1a8b368f